description:
Location: `crate-jmap-mail-server/src/sieve.rs:1139-1178`

  pub async fn handle_sieve_validate<B: MailBackend + SieveBackend>(
      backend: &B,
      args: Value,
  ) -> Result<(Value, Vec<Invocation>), JmapError> {
      let account_id = ...;
      let blob_id: Id = ...;
      if !backend.account_exists(&account_id).await? { return Err(JmapError::account_not_found()); }
      // Step 4: Delegate validation to the backend.
      let validation_error = backend
          .validate_sieve_script(&account_id, &blob_id)
          .await...
      ...
  }

CONCERN
The `SieveBackend::validate_sieve_script` trait doc (sieve.rs:67-71) explicitly tells implementors:

  /// Note: this method validates script syntax only. Script blob size should
  /// be checked separately using [`SieveBackend::max_sieve_script_bytes`]
  /// before calling this method.

The `handle_sieve_set` create path (sieve.rs:505-529) and update path (sieve.rs:680-705) BOTH read `max_sieve_script_bytes`, fetch the blob, and short-circuit with a `tooLarge` SetError before calling `validate_sieve_script`. The validate handler does NOT.

This means: an authenticated client can call `SieveScript/validate` with the `blobId` of an arbitrarily large blob (no requirement it be a script destined for storage) and force the backend to parse it. The trait doc says size-check happens at the handler layer; the handler skips that step for the validate path. A backend author who followed the trait contract assumes the handler enforces size — which is true for set, false for validate — and ships an unbounded Sieve-parser invocation.

Real Sieve parsers can be slow (O(n²) on certain pattern inputs, especially with nested `anyof` / `allof` rules), so the DoS amplification is meaningful: a 10 MiB blob with adversarial structure can hold a tokio worker for seconds.

WHAT GOES WRONG
DoS (CPU). Authenticated client can force the backend to parse arbitrarily large blobs as Sieve scripts. The handler bypasses the size limit that the JMAP server advertised in its `maxSizeScript` capability — violating RFC 9661 §2.6 advertisement consistency.

PROPOSED FIX
Add the size-check block before the validate call, mirroring `handle_sieve_set` lines 505-529:

  let max_script_bytes: Option<u64> = backend
      .max_sieve_script_bytes(&account_id)
      .await
      .map_err(|e| JmapError::server_fail(e.to_string()))?;
  if let Some(max_bytes) = max_script_bytes {
      match backend.get_sieve_blob(&account_id, &blob_id).await {
          Ok(Some(ref bytes)) if bytes.len() as u64 > max_bytes => {
              // Build a typed 'tooLarge' validation-error response and short-circuit.
              return Ok((json!({
                  "accountId": account_id.as_ref(),
                  "error": json!({ "type": "tooLarge", "description": format!("script exceeds maxSizeScript limit of {max_bytes} bytes") })
              }), vec![]));
          }
          Ok(_) => {}
          Err(e) => return Err(JmapError::server_fail(e.to_string())),
      }
  }

Add a test that posts a blob exceeding `max_sieve_script_bytes` and confirms the validate handler returns `tooLarge` without invoking `validate_sieve_script`.

ORACLE
- RFC 9661 §2.6: the validate method MUST observe `maxSizeScript`. The handler's job is to enforce capability limits before delegating to backend parsing.
- The SieveBackend trait doc itself at sieve.rs:67-71 — the contract that the handler is currently violating.

RISK CLASSIFICATION
P2. Authenticated CPU-DoS on Sieve-enabled deployments. The fix is mechanical — copy the existing size-check block from set to validate.
